³1revision³16³1text_default³1version³21³2ts³21100684076³2ip³2217.247.143.218³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Utilitarianism and <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> =

&nbsp;


Utilitarianism. Two types-- General Utilitarianism & Rule Utilitarianism (utilitism). See [http://www.hazlitt.org/e-texts/morality/ The Foundations of Morality] by [http://www.hazlitt.org/name.html Henry Hazlitt].

Generally, if one can predict a net gain in utility (to self or to the human race), then the action is to be taken...regardless of any moral stance. Or rulewise, if one can devise a rule that if everyone abided by that rule, one can predict results of a net gain in utility (to the rule abiders or the human race), then the action is to be taken...regardless of any moral stance.

The trick of predicting or defining 'utility' is said to be the weakness here. But, remember that day-to-day, humans do that anyway as best they can. One cannot predict to the end of time. And in complex, mutlivariate situations, too often prejudice or wishful thinking comes into play. In any case, read Hazlitt as he might make more sense of it.

